    m = 1e-17 eV, spin 0.03: v_rot = 8.5 km/s, l_v = 26.6 pc (4.4e+05 vortices inside 10 kpc), lambda_dB = 8.52e-03 pc;  delta K/K: lattice 2.5e-14, tangle 1.4e-16
    m = 1e-17 eV, spin 0.05: v_rot = 14.1 km/s, l_v = 20.6 pc (7.4e+05 vortices inside 10 kpc), lambda_dB = 8.52e-03 pc;  delta K/K: lattice 3.3e-14, tangle 1.4e-16
    m = 1e-15 eV, spin 0.03: v_rot = 8.5 km/s, l_v = 2.7 pc (4.4e+07 vortices inside 10 kpc), lambda_dB = 8.52e-05 pc;  delta K/K: lattice 2.5e-15, tangle 1.4e-18
    m = 1e-15 eV, spin 0.05: v_rot = 14.1 km/s, l_v = 2.1 pc (7.4e+07 vortices inside 10 kpc), lambda_dB = 8.52e-05 pc;  delta K/K: lattice 3.3e-15, tangle 1.4e-18
  [PASS] S2 at the lattice scale the gate channel only modulates FL2's conversion shift between 0 (cores) and its maximum, below CV4's 4.8e-3 for c_2 >= 1e-3 at z <= 4; the swirl's own moving-pattern channel (kernel-invisible: G_1 at C = 0 ~ alpha_c/c_2) is below 1e-10 for the lattice and the random tangle
         measured: gate channel by c_2: {'1e-04': '1.0e-02', '1e-03': '1.1e-03', '3e-03': '3.8e-04', '7e-03': '1.5e-04'}; swirl channel worst 2.3e-13
         reading:  the swirl adds nothing measurable to K; the only lattice-scale structure in K is the conversion gate's, which has a hole at every core.  At the KM1 floor c_2 = 1e-4 the conversion's own shift reaches 1.0e-2 at z = 4 (FL2 V4), above CV4's moving-source number -- a statement about the conversion, not the swirl

==============================================================================================================
S3  NO VORTICITY REACHES tau AT LINEAR ORDER: K and the lapse ignore a transverse shift (a longitudinal one enters)
==============================================================================================================
    linear K, no shift: -3*Phi(t, x, y, z)*Derivative(a(t), t)/a(t) - 3*Derivative(Psi(t, x, y, z), t) - Derivative(pi(t, x, y, z), (x, 2))/a(t)**2 - Derivative(pi(t, x, y, z), (y, 2))/a(t)**2 - Derivative(pi(t, x, y, z), (z, 2))/a(t)**2
    change from a transverse shift B = curl(chi z^): 0;  lapse change: 0
    change from a longitudinal shift B = grad chi (control): -Derivative(chi(t, x, y, z), (x, 2)) - Derivative(chi(t, x, y, z), (y, 2)) - Derivative(chi(t, x, y, z), (z, 2))  (= -1 x lap chi)
    the khronon normal's twist, curl(grad tau) = 0 (hypersurface-orthogonal by construction)
    second order: |B| ~ (v/c) Phi/c^2 = 3.0e-10; a B dB term gives delta K/K ~ 1.3e-14, a (dB)^2 source against the K^2 stiffness ~ 1.9e-09 at r = 10 kpc (upper estimates)
  [PASS] S3 at linear order the khronon's K and the lapse are independent of a transverse (vortical) shift, while a longitudinal shift does enter K (the control); the khronon's normal is twist-free; V0's khronon terms are a^2 and (K - <K>)^2 only -- so no vorticity coupling reaches tau at linear order
         measured: transverse dK 0, dN 0; longitudinal dK = -1 lap chi; twist 0
         reading:  the fluid's swirl sources only the metric's gravitomagnetic (vector) part, which the clock's scalar equations never see at this order; the second-order leak is negligible

==============================================================================================================
S4  CRITERION B: the circulation lives in the order parameter's phase; tau stays single-valued; no fold
==============================================================================================================
    loop of radius 1.55 l_v encloses 7 psi_H cores: fluid circulation = 7.0000 h/m (one quantum per core); the clock's loop integral = -2.89e-08 (of grad pi, against its natural scale 6.93e+00)
    the khronon's tilt pi solved from the swirling fluid's E_int (lap pi = -E_int, periodic): single-valued by construction, max |grad pi| = 0.712 (finite, smooth through every core)
    |grad psi|^2 on loops of radius 1e-3 and 1e-5 around a core: [(177.6247, 177.6247), (177.6604, 177.6604)] (continuous: True)
  [PASS] S4 the fluid's circulation is quantised in its phase (N h/m around N cores, mean vorticity 2 Omega) while the loop integral of the khronon's tilt -- solved from the swirling fluid's own E_int -- is zero: tau is single-valued, its source is smooth (S1) and the stress is continuous through every core, so the leaves never fold
         measured: fluid 7.000 h/m around 7 cores; clock -2.9e-08 (scale 6.9e+00); stress continuous True
         reading:  the dark fluid can swirl without disturbing the slices: its swirl is the winding of its own phase (a branch cut in theta is harmless because psi is single-valued); a clock made of the fluid's dust would carry that winding and fold

==============================================================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================================================
  Yes: the dark fluid can swirl without disturbing the clock's slices.  A rotating halo's fluid carries its angular
  momentum in quantised vortices (l_v ~ 188 pc for m = 2e-19 eV at spin 0.03, falling as m^-1/2;
  the random tangle's lambda_dB is ~0.4 pc).  At every core the gate's khronon source vanishes smoothly (S1).  The swirl's own
  channel into K is below 1e-10, and the lattice only modulates FL2's conversion shift between 0 and its maximum (S2).  At
  linear order no vorticity reaches tau, because the clock is twist-free and its K and lapse ignore the gravitomagnetic
  field (S3).  The circulation lives in the order parameter's phase, so tau stays single-valued (S4).
  Time 5 s.

  4/4 checks pass; load-bearing failures: 0; wrote FL3_swirl_and_the_clock_results.json
